hance your  chances of completing an operational  mission if you get involved with  hostilities what else did we do  we collected intelligence on Soviet  submarines during the Cold War the  Soviets tried to convince the world that  they had 400 to 500 submarines we would  fly in the Sea of Japan and we would see  the Russians changing the whole numbers  on the side of the submarines because I  don't remember in World War 2 when the  British built the plywood Navy so when  the Germans would fly over and they take  pictures of you know they thought they  had something that they really didn't  have so we would get information on on  Soviet submarines we'd get information  on Soviet ships one time I happen to be  out and our crew ran smack-dab in the  middle of a Soviet ocean exercise that  basically was probably an ASW exercise  but we photographed whisky class  submarines we were one of the first  quadrants to photograph the golf class  submarines which was a ballistic missile  submarine so we got quite a bit of the  order of battle photographed typically  when we got jumped the intercepts would  not last that long they would go  anywhere from maybe seven to ten minutes  keep in mind that a patrol plane that's  Pacala driven is more efficient at lower  altitudes and a jet is more efficient at  higher altitude so if we would go low  then of course we would have an  advantage also in the most serious  incident that we had we were jumped by a  MIG out of North Korea and they had  their fire control radar on us which  means they were ready to shoot us down  but it was overcast and we were able to  go into the soup I've always told people  we went below  500 feet we probably were 50 to 75 feet  off the deck now the reason that we did  that is because it's very difficult for  a jet flying at the speed that they  would fly to pull up and if they you  know impact the water they would run  their entire afternoon  so we basically would use that as a  defense tactic again I'd like you to  think about government policy Defense  Department policy in not going after  downed Airmen now there are some reasons  which I wouldn't support but typical  scenario might be intelligence it's  really hard that says there are nine  people in a prison camp north of a place  called Seca Virage in the Soviet Far  East  and you're doing an extraction you know  proposal and it says well it's gonna  cost you 50 people to get nine out and  also you might risk war so you have  these so-called whiz kids up in the  Pentagon saying well you know this  doesn't make sense I wouldn't want to be  in the position to make that decision  because myself I would go for it and say  well we're just gonna have to kill more  of them than we lose and the United  States well as long as we're exist those  situations are going to come up I would  encourage you to read the book because I  think there's some serious policy  questions that are raised not just about  the whole issue of extractions but also  about the need to continue to exercise  vigilance and going against a potential  enemy here we have a situation now where  we can use non pilot aircraft to collect  intelligence let me just close by saying  I was very lucky after the two squadron  tours and the Naval Postgraduate School  I got has got us back to the Defense  Intelligence Agency headquarters again  you get a lot more of the big picture  and there we I couldn't say this without  probably getting shot or arrested back  in 1965  we use the the we use satellite  photography I was a chief target analyst  wall of Soviet labor installations so  words I mean I when I went through  college I never thought I could  pronounce Vladivostok more let's look at  it but look so you know places like  Vladivostok and Olga Bay and Petri and  you know Sevastopol and all these other  places you that's what you're looking at  that was an exciting position to have  because I think that the United States  from a technology standpoint has really  done an incredible job in staying ahead  of the curve notwithstanding recent  glitches in the era of human  intelligence but from a technology side  I think that countries made a tremendous  leap and professor burrows wrote deep  black which goes onto the even side of  that out of the National Security Agency  so I'd encourage you to read this raises  a lot of issues about policy I think the  people that did this and I say  particularly the families the wives and  the children the anxiety that they  experienced I think puts them in the  hero category in heroine category  because they had to live in a situation  where their fathers and their husbands  couldn't share with them what they were  doing and one of the things about life  is you know meaning what you do is of  great significance so imagine that so  basically that's all I have to say it is  quite a lot what's been going through  our mind right now is that this has  amounted with the two of you here this  is amounted to an oral history and very  valuable in its own sake I would say a  couple more things in closing one is  this is the tenth book I've written and  in the course of the other nine I have  made a lot of really great acquaintances  this remains the only book I've ever  written where I have made lasting  friends and for that I am deeply  grateful of to you people finally I  would like to say that this ain't just  history it is current events  when Shane Osborn ran into our vice  versa  that authentic Chinese hot dog here at  Hainan Island on April 1st and had to  land with his crew that he was on one of  these missions the Navy was flying it  and it's important to note that as soon  as those people were released by the  Chinese the Air Force rivet joint  program picked it up to show the other  side that we weren't going to get chased  out of there that the rate that the  reconnaissance was just as important now  as it was during the Cold War and what  you're not reading coming out of  Afghanistan is we're still doing it and  as an old newsman I can't help but make  the remark that when you lose 30 or 40  planes on a mission that's news as when  a house falls down that's news when you  don't lose any planes on a mission  that's not news but some of us know that  the reason our losses have been very  light against Iraq and in Kosovo in 99  and now in Afghanistan is because the  recce guys the intelligence collectors  were there first flying the shadow  missions out of sight of the rest of the  world and with no recognition at all so  they're still doing it and a lot of  lives  are owed to these very brave very quiet  heroes and I guess I guess that's it  thank you
 

